1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,338
Picture a rising line on a dashboard: messages per session, up twenty percent month

2
00:00:05,338 --> 00:00:05,999
over month.

3
00:00:05,999 --> 00:00:09,064
For most features, that line is an unambiguous win.

4
00:00:09,064 --> 00:00:12,008
Ship it to the team, put it in the deck, move on.

5
00:00:12,008 --> 00:00:12,910
Sound familiar?

6
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:17,657
For an AI feature, that same line has two completely different honest readings.

7
00:00:17,657 --> 00:00:21,263
People might be getting real value and coming back for more.

8
00:00:21,263 --> 00:00:26,311
Or the assistant might be getting it wrong on the first try often enough that people

9
00:00:26,311 --> 00:00:31,239
are re-typing the same request three different ways before it lands, and every one

10
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:33,522
of those retries counts as engagement.

11
00:00:33,522 --> 00:00:38,570
Generic product metrics were built assuming a feature does the same thing every time

12
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:39,171
it's used.

13
00:00:39,171 --> 00:00:44,159
An AI feature doesn't, and that single difference is why this lecture exists: three

14
00:00:44,159 --> 00:00:48,906
metrics built specifically for a feature that behaves differently every time it

15
00:00:48,906 --> 00:00:49,207
runs.

16
00:00:49,207 --> 00:00:52,212
None of this means engagement is a useless number.

17
00:00:52,212 --> 00:00:57,139
It means engagement alone was never built to answer the question you actually care

18
00:00:57,139 --> 00:01:00,505
about: is this feature good, or is it merely being used.

19
00:01:00,505 --> 00:01:05,132
Those are different questions, and only a probabilistic feature makes the gap

20
00:01:05,132 --> 00:01:06,514
between them this wide.

21
00:01:07,064 --> 00:01:09,542
Read each one against what it replaces.

22
00:01:09,542 --> 00:01:14,753
Adoption replaces raw usage counts, which a small group of power users can inflate

23
00:01:14,753 --> 00:01:17,295
while everyone else ignores the feature.

24
00:01:17,295 --> 00:01:22,252
It asks the harder question: of everyone who had this available, what fraction

25
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:23,840
actually chose to use it.

26
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,827
That denominator is what teams skip most often.

27
00:01:26,827 --> 00:01:31,720
"Ten thousand people used the feature" means something different depending on

28
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,279
whether ten thousand or two hundred thousand could have.

29
00:01:35,279 --> 00:01:40,299
Skipping the eligible population is the same mistake as an average with no tail

30
00:01:40,299 --> 00:01:41,570
check underneath it.

31
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:46,590
Acceptance leans hardest on production: the closest thing to a live, continuous

32
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:51,547
version of 4.2's golden set, scored by every real user instead of fifty chosen

33
00:01:51,547 --> 00:01:51,928
cases.

34
00:01:51,928 --> 00:01:56,631
Someone keeping a drafted email as written, or rewriting three of its five

35
00:01:56,631 --> 00:01:59,745
sentences, is a quality signal arriving for free.

36
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:04,956
Deflection matters most for the feature this course keeps returning to: the refund

37
00:02:04,956 --> 00:02:08,324
agent, handling a request that used to need a person.

38
00:02:08,324 --> 00:02:12,645
A rising conversation count says nothing about whether it's working.

39
00:02:12,645 --> 00:02:17,792
Deflection says exactly what matters: of the refund requests that reached it, how

40
00:02:17,792 --> 00:02:20,715
many closed without a human touching the case.

41
00:02:20,715 --> 00:02:25,799
That's the number 7.2's cost model and 8.6's incident plan were quietly building

42
00:02:25,799 --> 00:02:30,438
toward, the one that finally says whether the automation earned its keep.

43
00:02:30,988 --> 00:02:34,256
Notice both columns start from the identical number.

44
00:02:34,256 --> 00:02:39,410
The only difference is whether anyone checked a second metric before deciding what

45
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:40,667
the first one meant.

46
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:45,695
An engagement number alone can't distinguish delight from friction, because both

47
00:02:45,695 --> 00:02:50,911
look identical in a raw usage count: more messages, more sessions, more time in the

48
00:02:50,911 --> 00:02:51,414
feature.

49
00:02:51,414 --> 00:02:56,568
Acceptance is the metric that tells the two apart, because a user delighted with a

50
00:02:56,568 --> 00:03:01,659
first answer doesn't need a second one, and a user fighting the feature generates

51
00:03:01,659 --> 00:03:06,373
exactly the retries that inflate engagement while acceptance quietly drops.

52
00:03:06,373 --> 00:03:11,652
This is the same discipline 7.5 asked of a margin number and an engagement number on

53
00:03:11,652 --> 00:03:12,532
the same page.

54
00:03:12,532 --> 00:03:16,114
Two metrics rising or falling together tell a real story.

55
00:03:16,114 --> 00:03:20,137
One metric alone is a single data point dressed up as an answer.

56
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:23,279
Notice, too, which team usually owns which column.

57
00:03:23,279 --> 00:03:28,496
Product tends to watch the engagement number by default, because it's the one every

58
00:03:28,496 --> 00:03:29,879
dashboard already has.

59
00:03:29,879 --> 00:03:35,158
Acceptance often has to be deliberately added, by someone who specifically asked for

60
00:03:35,158 --> 00:03:40,123
it, which is exactly why so many teams are flying with only the first column in

61
00:03:40,123 --> 00:03:40,437
view.

62
00:03:40,987 --> 00:03:45,808
Put the two cards from the last slide into one grid and the point gets sharper.

63
00:03:45,808 --> 00:03:50,934
The top row is identical wherever you look: messages per session, up twenty percent,

64
00:03:50,934 --> 00:03:54,107
in both readings, because that number never changed.

65
00:03:54,107 --> 00:03:57,463
The bottom row is where the two readings stop agreeing.

66
00:03:57,463 --> 00:04:02,222
Read across the first column and acceptance simply isn't there, because nobody

67
00:04:02,222 --> 00:04:02,832
pulled it.

68
00:04:02,832 --> 00:04:07,897
Read across the second and it's down eight points, sitting right next to the twenty

69
00:04:07,897 --> 00:04:09,972
percent rise it was hiding behind.

70
00:04:09,972 --> 00:04:13,206
Same release, same underlying data, one column apart.

71
00:04:13,206 --> 00:04:16,745
The gap between those two cells isn't a measurement error.

72
00:04:16,745 --> 00:04:21,748
It's the entire cost of skipping a second metric before you write "clear win" in a

73
00:04:21,748 --> 00:04:22,603
weekly update.

74
00:04:23,153 --> 00:04:28,047
Say the honest caveat, because it's tempting to treat acceptance as the one metric

75
00:04:28,047 --> 00:04:32,584
that finally can't be misread, and it can be, in the opposite direction from

76
00:04:32,584 --> 00:04:33,240
engagement.

77
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:38,254
A suggestion accepted without being read produces the exact same acceptance event as

78
00:04:38,254 --> 00:04:41,478
a suggestion read carefully and judged genuinely good.

79
00:04:41,478 --> 00:04:46,372
Low-stakes features, an autocomplete suggestion nobody's scrutinising closely, are

80
00:04:46,372 --> 00:04:47,864
especially prone to this.

81
00:04:47,864 --> 00:04:52,878
High acceptance on a feature where the cost of being wrong is low is weaker evidence

82
00:04:52,878 --> 00:04:56,519
than the identical number on a feature people actually check.

83
00:04:56,519 --> 00:05:01,295
This is 4.9's discipline again, aimed at a new number: don't trust one metric in

84
00:05:01,295 --> 00:05:03,264
isolation, however good it looks.

85
00:05:03,264 --> 00:05:08,040
Pair acceptance with a downstream signal where you can get one: did the accepted

86
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:12,994
draft get sent unedited, did the accepted suggestion actually get used five minutes

87
00:05:12,994 --> 00:05:15,143
later, or did it quietly get undone.

88
00:05:15,143 --> 00:05:18,008
The fix isn't abandoning acceptance as a metric.

89
00:05:18,008 --> 00:05:23,022
It's the same fix as everywhere else in this course: pair a number with a second one

90
00:05:23,022 --> 00:05:27,857
before trusting either alone, and be specific about what would make you doubt the

91
00:05:27,857 --> 00:05:32,751
first number rather than simply believing it because it moved the right direction.

92
00:05:33,301 --> 00:05:38,276
That's the floor worth building before any other analytics work in this section, and

93
00:05:38,276 --> 00:05:43,073
the definition's last clause is the one worth remembering under pressure: none of

94
00:05:43,073 --> 00:05:45,264
the three, alone, is the whole story.

95
00:05:45,264 --> 00:05:48,344
Run the comparison on real numbers if you have them.

96
00:05:48,344 --> 00:05:53,318
Most teams have engagement instrumented already and acceptance instrumented nowhere,

97
00:05:53,318 --> 00:05:58,056
which is itself the finding: the metric this section calls the most important is

98
00:05:58,056 --> 00:06:00,425
usually the one nobody's collecting yet.

99
00:06:00,425 --> 00:06:05,341
Notice this floor doesn't require new infrastructure to define, only to instrument,

100
00:06:05,341 --> 00:06:07,709
which is exactly next lecture's subject.

101
00:06:07,709 --> 00:06:11,144
Knowing what to measure is the cheap half of this problem.

102
00:06:11,144 --> 00:06:15,823
Actually firing the event at the right moment, for every user, is the part most

103
00:06:15,823 --> 00:06:17,007
teams underestimate.

104
00:06:17,557 --> 00:06:19,313
One thing before you move on.

105
00:06:19,313 --> 00:06:21,552
Check for acceptance first, honestly.

106
00:06:21,552 --> 00:06:26,152
If the answer is that no event fires when a user keeps, edits, or discards a

107
00:06:26,152 --> 00:06:31,176
suggestion, that's today's finding, and it's a bigger gap than a missing engagement

108
00:06:31,176 --> 00:06:32,144
metric would be.

109
00:06:32,144 --> 00:06:37,047
If you have both numbers, put them side by side before trusting either one alone.

110
00:06:37,047 --> 00:06:41,828
Both of these are conversations you can start today, before any new code ships.

111
00:06:41,828 --> 00:06:46,912
The instrumentation next lecture covers is what turns the conversation into a number

112
00:06:46,912 --> 00:06:48,304
you can actually trust.

113
00:06:48,304 --> 00:06:53,389
Next lecture is the instrumentation plan: exactly which events to fire, and when, so

114
00:06:53,389 --> 00:06:58,291
acceptance, adoption, and deflection are numbers you have on day one, not numbers

115
00:06:58,291 --> 00:07:00,773
you retrofit after someone asks for them.
